<h2 id="aprendizaje">What I learned <i class="fas fa-graduation-cap"></i> </h2>
<p>
• React — I learned to build the application using reusable views and components, which makes the site tidier and easier to maintain. <br />
• Context and state — I brought in context and state variables to handle global information such as the user and the cart, making changes show up across the whole application without a reload. <br />
• useEffect — I used this hook to run automatic actions, such as fetching data from the backend or keeping the user session alive if they were already logged in. <br />
• Protected routes — I implemented routes that only logged-in users can see and others exclusive to admins, ensuring security and access control. <br />
• Notifications — I integrated toasts to give the user immediate feedback on their actions (for example, changing quantities or confirming purchases). <br />
• Full integration — I connected the React frontend with the Node.js backend and the MySQL database, achieving a complete system that covers both the customer experience and admin management. <br />
• Database diagrams — I learned to design complete database diagrams using draw.io, which let me visualize and better organize the relationships between tables and entities. <br />
• Database seeds — I adopted the practice of creating seeds to empty the database or fill it with sample data, making development, testing and validation of the system easier. <br />
</p>
